<h2>Accounts</h2>
<p>Under <strong>Administration → Accounts</strong>: search, block and unblock, mark as verified, send a password reset, change roles, delete. The page of an account shows its log.</p>
<p><strong>Create account</strong> makes an account for someone, with or without a password. Without one, the person gets an e-mail with a link to set their own.</p>

<h2>Signing in as this account</h2>
<p>With the permission <code>core.accounts.impersonate</code>, the site can be shown the way this account sees it - to find out what the person actually sees. A bar stays at the top of every page until <strong>End and back to the administration</strong> is clicked. While signed in as someone else, the password, the e-mail address and the account itself cannot be changed or deleted. Administrators cannot be entered this way, nor one's own account, nor a blocked one. Every creation and every sign-in of this kind is kept in the account's log.</p>

<h2>Roles</h2>
<p>Under <strong>Administration → Roles</strong>: a role is a set of permissions. Permissions are grouped like the menu. Three presets are offered: editor, moderator and support. Handing out roles hands out permissions too, so changing roles needs a permission of its own. The administrator role cannot be changed or deleted.</p>

<h2>Permissions at a glance</h2>
<table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Permission</th><th scope="col">What it allows</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>core.admin.access</code></td><td>to open the administration at all</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>core.pages.manage</code></td><td>content pages</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>core.media.manage</code></td><td>the media library</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>core.offers.manage</code>, <code>core.categories.manage</code>, <code>core.providers.manage</code></td><td>the catalogue</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>core.orders.manage</code></td><td>orders and withdrawals</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>core.reviews.manage</code>, <code>core.reports.manage</code></td><td>moderation</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>core.accounts.manage</code>, <code>core.accounts.impersonate</code></td><td>people; signing in as someone</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>core.roles.manage</code></td><td>roles</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>core.settings.manage</code></td><td>settings, modules, payment methods, design, home and offer page, word filter</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>core.themes.manage</code>, <code>core.packages.manage</code>, <code>core.extensions.manage</code></td><td>system</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>core.update.manage</code>, <code>core.tasks.view</code></td><td>updates and tasks</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
<p>The first administrator account is created during setup and holds every permission (<code>*</code>).</p>
